fixer_verifier_output.raw_output:
Root cause: use-after-free occurs because the code path that frees a closure's function_name in the zend_closure_free_storage path is malformed (brace/flow issue) causing a zend_string_release to run on a freed string. Minimal fix: restore the proper structure of the else-if branch and guard the release with a NULL check, ensuring we release only non-interned function_name once and then set it to NULL. Patch (conceptual):

In src/php-src/Zend/zend_closures.c, within zend_closure_free_storage, replace the problematic block around lines 485-491 with:

} else if (closure->orig_internal_handler == zend_closure_call_magic) {
    if (closure->func.common.function_name && !ZSTR_IS_INTERNED(closure->func.common.function_name)) {
        zend_string_release(closure->func.common.function_name);
    }
    closure->func.common.function_name = NULL;
}

This restores the intended control flow: the else-if is properly closed, the release happens only when the name is heap-allocated, and we clear the pointer to avoid double-free. This should eliminate the heap-use-after-free reported by AddressSanitizer when closures are destroyed. 

Notes:
- The fix is minimal, localized to the destructor path, and does not impact functional behavior otherwise.
- No tests were modified; just the non-test source file. 

If you want, I can generate a concrete git patch diff as well.

